WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.430
As TV audiences continue to fracture, the
definition of a hit show is constantly evolving,

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.549
and with every success, there's always a clunker
waiting to be axed.

00:00:09.549 --> 00:00:13.980
Some critics feel that 2017 has brought some
of the worst shows in recent history.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:19.140
With that in mind, we've rounded up the eight
worst shows of 2017.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.080
Emerald City

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:26.210
Just a few years ago, Uproxx reported on five
vastly different Wizard of Oz TV adaptations

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.320
in development.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.939
Only one of those made it to air.

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:33.190
Emerald City was director Tarsem Singh's first
foray into television.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:37.660
It was clear that NBC was hoping for their
own Game of Thrones, but while Emerald City

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.750
was both beautiful and violent, no amount
of Singh's signature special effects could

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.800
compensate for a weak storyline.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.220
According to The Hollywood Reporter,

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:50.030
"...the silly merges with the violent, fumbling
the tone on down the Yellow Brick Road (which

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:51.520
here is made of opium)."

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:57.120
Even its stellar cast - featuring Adria Arjona,
Oliver Jackson-Cohen, and Vincent D'Onofrio

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.820
- was not enough to keep audiences interested.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.620
With all those effects, the show couldn't
have been cheap—which meant that low ratings

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.150
quickly doomed the 10-episode season.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:11.350
After premiering the show in January, NBC
canceled Emerald City in May.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:12.810
APB

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:17.480
When sci-fi satire APB premiered on Fox, it
had a lot of potential.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:21.730
The series took aim at America's police state
in the making—but it felt more like a comedy

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.810
than the drama it really needed to be.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:28.780
APB followed tech billionaire Gideon Reeves,
played by Justin Kirk, and explored privately

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.470
funded police forces equipped with drones,
mobile apps, and tech gadgets.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:38.409
However, too much focus was put on flashy
technology, leaving no room for actual character

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:39.409
development.

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:40.409
The Hollywood Reporter called the show

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:43.020
"Just an empty, run-of-the-mill cop show."

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.150
"It's just stupid."

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:49.120
Heavily relying on style over substance, APB
buckled under its own distorted morality.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:55.550
Earning a 35 percent rating at Rotten Tomatoes,
it was canceled after 12 episodes.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:56.550
Taken

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:01.820
NBC tried to tap into a particular set of
skills with Taken—a prequel series to Liam

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:07.050
Neeson's popular film franchise--but unfortunately,
Neeson had nothing to do with this spy drama.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.830
"I don't know who you are."

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:14.019
Clive Standen stars as a young Bryan Mills,
giving audiences a perspective into the past

00:02:14.019 --> 00:02:16.500
of Neeson's hardened espionage agent.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.779
Regardless of its prequel status, Taken was
ultimately just a procedural that struggled

00:02:20.779 --> 00:02:25.180
to live up to the kinetic pacing and compelling
action of the movies that inspired it.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:26.579
As The Hollywood Reporter explained:

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:31.739
"What Taken actually proves to be as a series
is just another NBC action drama in which

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:35.040
a government team faces a different limited
threat each week."

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.299
Poor reviews aside, Taken performed well enough,
prompting NBC to order a Season 2.

00:02:40.299 --> 00:02:44.549
According to Deadline, only Jennifer Beals
and Standen will return—which means some

00:02:44.549 --> 00:02:49.620
well-needed restructuring has definitely TAKEN
place.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:50.939
Training Day

00:02:50.939 --> 00:02:56.389
While Taken was renewed for a second season
at NBC, CBS's small-screen adaptation of Antoine

00:02:56.389 --> 00:02:59.200
Fuqua's Training Day wasn't so lucky.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.109
Coming in at 22% on Rotten Tomatoes, the show
was plagued with horrible reviews as soon

00:03:04.109 --> 00:03:05.109
as it premiered.

00:03:05.109 --> 00:03:06.109
Vulture called the show a

00:03:06.109 --> 00:03:10.950
"...hackneyed, dreadfully written carbon copy
of every crummy cop show you've ever seen."

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:16.390
Flipping the script on the 2001 film's original
story, the series paired rookie Kyle Craig,

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:21.219
played by Justin Cornwell, with seasoned officer
Frank Rourke, played by Bill Paxton.

00:03:21.219 --> 00:03:26.329
It was an intriguing but risky move for CBS,
who relied mostly on Paxton's acting chops

00:03:26.329 --> 00:03:27.739
to carry the series.

00:03:27.739 --> 00:03:32.340
But Paxton's talents couldn't save Training
Day from overused exposition and slow pace.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:37.459
The show was at risk as soon as it premiered,
and after Paxton's untimely passing in February,

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:41.609
TV LINE reporter that it was moved from its
Thursday night time slot to Saturday.

00:03:41.609 --> 00:03:46.520
Deadline then reported that Training Day was
officially canceled months later.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:47.560
Star

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:51.849
Due to the massive success of Empire, Fox
brought another hip-hop-themed drama from

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:54.829
Oscar-nominated director Lee Daniels to the
small-screen.

00:03:54.829 --> 00:03:59.110
But audiences quickly discovered Star was
nothing like the network's lavish Lyon family

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:00.110
drama.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:04.510
The series follows the melodramatic aspirations
of Star Davis as she aims to get famous—or

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:05.569
die trying.

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:09.489
With scandalous storylines abound, Star might
not have been granted a second season without

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:12.200
big-name talent like Benjamin Bratt and Queen
Latifah.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:17.590
The one-dimensional characters, cheesy storylines,
and over-the-top music video segments make

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.840
it difficult to take Star seriously.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.030
The guilty pleasure angle may be the show's
selling point, setting up Star as a fun chaser

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.810
to the more serious Empire.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:29.919
According to Variety, a two-hour crossover
between the two shows helped boost Star's

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:31.379
second season ratings.

00:04:31.379 --> 00:04:35.789
But only time will tell if it will make it
to a third season.

00:04:35.789 --> 00:04:36.789
Disjointed

00:04:36.789 --> 00:04:40.810
Chuck Lorre knows a thing or two about sitcom
success, bringing Two and a Half Men and The

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:45.530
Big Bang Theory to CBS—two comedies that
were regularly panned by critics while still

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.320
garnering huge ratings.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:52.020
Moving from network TV to Netflix's streaming
platform, Lorre has continued doing his thing

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.750
with the pot comedy Disjointed.

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:59.020
The show stars Kathy Bates as Ruth Whitefeather
Feldman, the owner of an offbeat medical marijuana

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:03.870
dispensary in Los Angeles, and used Lorre's
signature three-camera setup to explore the

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.680
misadventures of the shop's staff.

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.320
"Stoned, I am getting!"

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:09.660
The Daily Dot reported:

00:05:09.669 --> 00:05:14.060
"Just as real nerds tend to reject the heightened,
shrill versions of themselves they see on

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:18.750
The Big Bang Theory, real pot smokers are
likely to reject or even be offended by their

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.259
portrayal on Disjointed."

00:05:20.259 --> 00:05:24.150
The series may be summer 2017's weirdest new
show.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:27.889
According to The Wrap, it was also the summer's
worst.

00:05:27.889 --> 00:05:29.270
Marvel's Iron Fist

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:33.740
Marvel and Netflix have proven to be a worthy
partnership over the past few years.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:38.389
From Daredevil to Jessica Jones, fans had
grown accustomed to edgy comic book stories

00:05:38.389 --> 00:05:40.990
mixed with a heaping helping of action and
intrigue.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:43.520
That all came to a halt with Marvel's Iron
Fist.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.060
The conflict came slow, the fight sequences
fell flat, and Finn Jones' performance as

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.870
Danny Rand failed to live up to expectations.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:55.439
Jessica Henwick's portrayal of Colleen Wing
breathed some much-needed life into the series,

00:05:55.439 --> 00:05:58.210
but it wasn't enough to lift Iron Fist out
of the muck.

00:05:58.210 --> 00:05:59.580
Indiewire said the series felt

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.770
"...incredibly inessential, even boring at
times.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:05.069
It just… didn't need to happen.

00:06:05.069 --> 00:06:08.949
Marvel could have taken a pass on this one,
or found a more interesting take on it."

00:06:08.949 --> 00:06:13.509
Entertainment Weekly reports that second season
is underway, promising a full-on comic-style

00:06:13.509 --> 00:06:16.129
team-up between Danny Rand and Luke Cage.

00:06:16.129 --> 00:06:19.980
Here's hoping their partnership can lift the
series out of the Netflix doldrums.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.620
"Sweet Christmas."

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:24.220
Marvel's Inhumans

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:29.710
Scoring just 10 percent on Rotten Tomatoes,
Inhumans comes in as Marvel's worst-ever comic

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:30.979
book adaptation.

00:06:30.979 --> 00:06:34.659
Indiewire called it, quote, "the worst thing
Marvel has done in decades."

00:06:34.659 --> 00:06:39.409
ABC backed the series with a Disney budget,
and even gave it an IMAX limited theatrical

00:06:39.409 --> 00:06:44.259
release, but not even the star power of Anson
Mount and Iwan Rheon seemed to help.

00:06:44.259 --> 00:06:49.430
Box Office Mojo reports just $2.9 million
in ticket sales for the theatrical release,

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:53.970
and TVSeriesFinale reports the show averaged
just under 2 million viewers.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:59.110
In this case, however, poor reviews and dismal
viewership might not lead to a cancellation.

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:03.660
Marvel's original plan for Inhumans called
for a big-screen MCU blockbuster, so Disney

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:06.690
might still greenlight more episodes on ABC's
behalf.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:10.699
On the bright side: if more seasons do come,
at least we'll get to see more of that cute

00:07:10.699 --> 00:07:11.699
giant dog.

00:07:11.699 --> 00:07:13.259
(Inhumans' only redeeming quality)

00:07:13.259 --> 00:07:14.479
Thanks for watching!

00:07:14.479 --> 00:07:17.180
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:19.580
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

